  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int 2x\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C\\in R$ is the arbitrary constant.\n\nMoreover, the differential equation ${f}^{″}(x)=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\int 2\\,dx \\\\  & =2x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D\\in R$ is another arbitrary constant.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇍\\,({f}^{″}(x)=2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ we have ${f}^{′}(x)=2x+1,$ and so the statement ${f}^{″}(x)=2$  is true but ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 2x.$\n\n- Condition II is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true *if and only if* $f(x)={x}^{2}+C,$ for any $C\\in R.$\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇒\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇐\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇔\\,(f(x)={x}^{2}+C)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Condition III is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({f}^{′}(x)=2x)\\,⇏\\,(f(x)={x}^{2})\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+1$ the statement ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ is true but $f(x)\\ne {x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
